/** @page grpcerrorsguide Error Handling

    @tableofcontents

    gRPC uses status codes for error communication via HTTP/2 trailers. The standard status
    codes are defined as constants:

    |!Constant|!Value|!Description
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_OK|0|Success
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_CANCELLED|1|Operation cancelled
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_UNKNOWN|2|Unknown error
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_INVALID_ARGUMENT|3|Invalid argument
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_DEADLINE_EXCEEDED|4|Deadline expired
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_NOT_FOUND|5|Entity not found
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_ALREADY_EXISTS|6|Entity already exists
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_PERMISSION_DENIED|7|Permission denied
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED|8|Resource exhausted
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_FAILED_PRECONDITION|9|Invalid state
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_ABORTED|10|Operation aborted
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_OUT_OF_RANGE|11|Out of range
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_UNIMPLEMENTED|12|Not implemented
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_INTERNAL|13|Internal error
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_UNAVAILABLE|14|Service unavailable
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_DATA_LOSS|15|Data loss
    |@ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_UNAUTHENTICATED|16|Unauthenticated

    Server handler exceptions are automatically caught and returned with the exception message as the status
    message.  The status code is derived from the exception code: \c GRPC-CANCELLED, \c GRPC-NOT-FOUND,
    \c GRPC-INVALID-ARGUMENT, \c GRPC-UNAUTHENTICATED, \c GRPC-PERMISSION-DENIED, \c GRPC-UNIMPLEMENTED,
    and \c GRPC-RESOURCE-EXHAUSTED map to the corresponding status, \c GRPC-TIMEOUT and \c FUTURE-TIMEOUT map to
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_DEADLINE_EXCEEDED, \c HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE maps to
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED (see @ref grpclimitsguide), and any other exception is returned as
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_INTERNAL.

    In a client-streaming or bidirectional call, the server can finish the call before the client has sent every
    message, for example by rejecting it.  A later @ref Grpc::GrpcClientStream::write() "write()" then raises
    \c GRPC-STREAM-ERROR, and @ref Grpc::GrpcClientStream::finish() "finish()" returns the status the server sent.
    @ref Grpc::GrpcClientStream::finish() "finish()" always returns a result: if the stream ends without a status from
    the server, such as when the server or a proxy resets the stream, the status is derived from the error; a reset is
    mapped by its HTTP/2 error code as in the gRPC protocol (\c REFUSED_STREAM to
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_UNAVAILABLE, \c CANCEL to @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_CANCELLED,
    \c ENHANCE_YOUR_CALM to @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED, \c INADEQUATE_SECURITY to
    @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_PERMISSION_DENIED, and any other code to @ref Qore::GRPC_STATUS_INTERNAL).
    A unary call whose stream is reset raises \c GRPC-ERROR.

    @code{.py}
GrpcClientStream stream = client.clientStream("Upload");
try {
    foreach hash<auto> record in (records) {
        stream.write(record);
    }
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err != "GRPC-STREAM-ERROR") {
        rethrow;
    }
    # the server finished the call early; finish() returns its status
}
hash<GrpcCallResult> result = stream.finish();
if (result.status_code != GRPC_STATUS_OK) {
    printf("upload failed: %d: %s\n", result.status_code, result.status_message);
}
    @endcode
*/
